UP|52|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|Let us discuss the thought behind 4@H. At a different vulnerability it might make sense. However, at unfavorable vulnerability (we are vulnerable, they are not) I think it is a tad too aggressive particularly since the @SK is unfavorably placed. Note that even if EW can make a game, -500 is a worse result for NS.
|
PG| ***** 22 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|As one of kibitzer suggested 3@H is a bit better. |
PG| ***** 23 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|As it turns out, E/W might make 6@C because of the very lucky diamond distribution. But you wouldn't want to be there because you could easily lose a diamond and a spade. And they also make 3N  because the diamond finesse works.|
PG| ***** 24 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at| 2@H by North would show a opening hand. It is just a standard overcall with a nice 6 card suit and close to opening values.On the other hand, 3@H is preemptive. But given I am a passed hand I think 3@H is fine|
PG| ***** 25 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^^B@2^*BA Kib: ^*NIs there any guidelines how many quick tricks one should have when preempting at 3/4 level or is it just length?|
PG| ***** 26 ***** |


cp|8|AT|^^
^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|I has more to do with length. However, if you have a flat hand (no singletons or voids) you should be a bit more cautious.|
PG| ***** 27 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|Basically how high you preempt has to do mostly with length (|CP|1|FP|2|AT|^*Hhttp://www.bridgesights.com/hondobridge/hondoviewer/hondoviewer.php?sf=lott,Law of Total Tricks^*N|CP||FP||at| is involved) and a little bit with distribution and whether or not you are vulnerable. |
PG| ***** 28 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|Be more aggressive with nice distribution and/or if not vulnerable (especially if they are vulnerable). Be more cautious with flat distribution or if vulnerable.|
PG| ***** 29 ***** |


