nt|^z@3^b@2^^^B@2^*BA Kib: ^*N Which do you prefer, semi or forcing 1NT? Also what happened with clubs signaling, do you prefer standard or UDCA?|
PG| ***** 50 ***** |


cp|8|AT|^^
^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|First, let me address ^*BForcing 1NT^*n. One of the treatments you use when you play 2/1 Game Forcing bidding is what is known as the ^*BForcing 1NT^*n. |
PG| ***** 51 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|That means when partner responds 1NT after an opening bid of 1@H or 1@S, opener may not pass. Sometimes that means that opener must rebid a 3-card minor (when opener is 5-3-3-2).|
PG| ***** 52 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|One of the important reasons behind playing 1NT forcing is that in order to show a 3-card limit (10-12) raise, responder first bids 1N and then makes a jump raise to 3 of opener’s major. Note that an immediate limit raise promises 4-card support.|
PG| ***** 53 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|Some pairs introduce the concept of the semi-forcing 1N to avoid having to bid a 3-card suit. That means that opener will occasionally pass a 1NT response if they have a minimum 5-3-3-2 hand. |
PG| ***** 54 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|When opener rebids a new suit, it will almost always promise a 4-card suit. Most pairs who play 1NT as semi-forcing only play it when responder is a passed hand. There are a few pairs that play semi-forcing 1NT even when responder is not a passed hand, but that is relatively rare.|
PG| ***** 55 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|The problem with playing 1NT as semi-forcing is when responder has a 3-card limit raise. In that case, if opener passes, you will end up playing in 1NT instead of your 5-3 major suit fit. That problem can be solved if you only play semi-forcing 1NT by a passed hand and you also play Drury. In that case, when responder has a 3-card limit raise, they bid 2@C.|
PG| ***** 56 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|I personally prefer playing 1NT as forcing even when responder is a passed hand. It gives you more flexibility for certain types of hands, such as weak hands with long suits (where responder bids 1N and then bids their suit to play). |
PG| ***** 57 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|I don't find there is usually a problem in those auctions where opener rebids a 3-card minor. But if you want to play 1NT as semi-forcing by a passed hand and you play Drury, that is very playable.|
PG| ***** 58 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|Next, is my view on UDCA Signals.|
PG| ***** 59 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|When signaling attitude on defense, most pairs play that a high spot is encouraging and a low spot is discouraging. When signaling count, you play high-low with an even number (often a doubleton) and low-high with an odd number.|
PG| ***** 60 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|Some pairs reverse the meaning of their signals. This is known as ^*B(UDCA)^*n--Upside Down Count and Attitude. The rationale behind UDCA is that, particularly with attitude signals, occasionally a defender may not be able to play a high spot to encourage because they need to preserve that high spot.|
PG| ***** 61 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|I am not a big fan of UDCA. It does normally work out okay with attitude. But it can be very confusing when playing count. To further complicate things, even pairs that play Upside Down Count will play standard signals when leading back remaining count (e.g. they will lead their high spot when they have 2 cards remaining in a suit and their low spot with 3 cards remaining). |
PG| ***** 62 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|So some pairs play upside down attitude and standard count, which to me is the worst of all possible worlds.|
PG| ***** 63 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|Having tried UDCA, I strongly recommend you stick with standard signals. They are easier and just as effective. There are only very rare instances where you cannot afford to encourage with a high spot, and that rare disadvantage is more than offset by the simplicity advantage of standard signals.|
PG| ***** 64 ***** |


