PG| ***** 66 ***** |


mb|P|mb|2H|nt|^z@3^b@2^^|mb|P?|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|Now, East is going  to use a rare convention that is very occasionally useful. 
It is called ^*BExclusion Key Card.^*N East should bid 5@C. That is Roman Key Card in hearts. It but asks partner not to count the @CA as a key card.|
PG| ***** 67 ***** |


mb|5C|nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|Responses to 5@C are as follows (assuming you play 0314):
5@D = 0 or 3 key cards;
5@H = 1 or 4 key cards;
5@S = 2 key cards so @SQ;
5N  = 2 key cards with the @SQ.
Note that the @CA is not counted as a key card when responding.|
PG| ***** 68 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^|
mb|P?|sk|w|
cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|On this hand West has 2 key cards because West has the @SA. But if she had the @CA instead of the @SA that would only be one key card.|
PG| ***** 69 ***** |


mb|5N|an|2 key cards and SQ|
PG| ***** 70 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^|sk|e|mb|P?|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|Now it is hard to know whether to bid 6 or 7. East can count 6 diamonds, 5 hearts, and a spade. But if West has 2 clubs, then East can count, with 2 club ruffs, 6 hearts (4 hearts in the West hand plus 2 club ruffs). So I would bid 7@H.
|
PG| ***** 71 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^^B@2^*BA Kib: ^*nHow does she know West has @SA? I'm missing that part of the logic. Sorry.|
PG| ***** 72 ***** |


cp|8|AT|^^
^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|Because the 5@C bid said West should not count the @CA as a key card. Since West showed 2 key cards, by bidding 5N, they must be the @HK and the @SA.|
PG| ***** 73 ***** |


