UP|52|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|Okay, This board illustrates when you should bid a grand. You should only bid a grand if you are pretty sure you can take 13 tricks. And on this hand I showed you how to count 13 tricks with 6 diamonds, 4 hearts in dummy, the @SA, and 2 club ruffs in the East hand.|
PG| ***** 86 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^^B@2^*BA Kib: ^*nWhat's the advantage of 3014 vs 1430 RKCBresponses to RKC?|
PG| ***** 87 ***** |


cp|8|AT|^^
^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|0314 is more logical than 1430, since a lower response logically should show fewer key cards than a higher response. Let me discuss why 1430 was invented and explain why it is not especially useful.|
PG| ***** 88 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|When partner responds with 0/3 or 1/4 key cards, sometimes the RKCB would like to know whether or not responder has the trump queen. This is accomplished by using the queen ask - 5@D over a 5@C response and 5@H over a 5@D response asks whether or not responder has the trump queen. |
PG| ***** 89 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|However, when hearts are trump, a 5@H bid over 5@D is to play and, therefore, the queen ask is not available. Someone decided it was more likely that you wanted to do the queen ask when responder had 1 key card than 0 key cards. |
PG| ***** 90 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|So they reversed the meaning of 5@C and 5@D (e.g. 1430) to allow for the queen ask when hearts are trump and responder had 1 key card. A very rare situation and rather dubious logic to justify an unneeded complication.|
PG| ***** 91 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|There is a much better solution to the queen ask problem. And that is to play |CP|1|FP|2|AT|^*Hhttp://www.bridgesights.com/hondobridge/hondoviewer/hondoviewer.php?sf=kickback,Kickback RKCBk^*N|CP||FP||at| where, when hearts are trump, 4@S is RKCB. Then, if responder bids 4N (0 or 3 key cards), 5@C is the queen ask. And if responder bids 5@C (1 or 4 key cards), 5@D is the queen ask. For that reason, I recommend you play 4@S as RKCB when hearts are trump.|
PG| ***** 92 ***** |


